[Handbook: Vascular Tumors]
The rare association of a vascular tumour with thrombocytopenia (platelet trapping in tumour) and sometimes microangiopathic haemolytic anaemia and a $2^{circ}$ consumptive coagulopathy.  The rare association of a vascular tumour with thrombocytopenia (platelet trapping in tumour) and sometimes microangiopathic haemolytic anaemia and a $2^{\circ}$ consumptive coagulopathy.  •    Occurs with some rare enlarging vascular tumours of infancy/childhood, e.g. tufted angioma and kaposiform haemangioendothelioma (not seen in infantile haemangioma). Podoplanin-positive endothelium in tumours binds CLEC-2 receptor on platelets, activates platelets and initiates clotting in tumour. •    Occurs with some rare enlarging vascular tumours of infancy/childhood, e.g. tufted angioma and kaposiform haemangioendothelioma (not seen in infantile haemangioma). Podoplanin-positive endothelium in tumours binds CLEC-2 receptor on platelets, activates platelets and initiates clotting in tumour.
Typically, this vascular tumour presents at the site of a penetrating injury, e.g. rose thorn, usually on the fingers, lips, or face (see Fig. 17.1).  Typically, this vascular tumour presents at the site of a penetrating injury, e.g. rose thorn, usually on the fingers, lips, or face (see Fig. 17.1).  • An amelanotic malignant melanoma or SCC may look like a pyogenic granuloma (see Fig. 17.11)—-always ask the patient if anything preceded the development of the tumour. Was there a ‘mole’ at the site, or did it arise on normal skin? Always send excised lesions for histological examination.  • An amelanotic malignant melanoma or SCC may look like a pyogenic granuloma (see Fig. 17.11)—-always ask the patient if anything preceded the development of the tumour. Was there a ‘mole’ at the site, or did it arise on normal skin? Always send excised lesions for histological examination.  What should I look for?  What should I look for?  •    A history of rapid growth: pyogenic granulomas grow on previously normal skin over a period of weeks to a size of $0.5{-}4mathsf{c m}$ . •     Fleshy vascular tumour composed of friable granulation tissue that bleeds readily on contact. •     Exclude any underlying pigmented lesion: inspect any ‘pyogenic granuloma’ at an atypical site particularly carefully, using a dermatoscope, if possible. Melanin can be mistaken for old haemorrhage.  •    A history of rapid growth: pyogenic granulomas grow on previously normal skin over a period of weeks to a size of $0.5{-}4\mathsf{c m}$ . •     Fleshy vascular tumour composed of friable granulation tissue that bleeds readily on contact. •     Exclude any underlying pigmented lesion: inspect any ‘pyogenic granuloma’ at an atypical site particularly carefully, using a dermatoscope, if possible. Melanin can be mistaken for old haemorrhage.


