[Handbook: Vasculitis Photos]
Vasculitis (angiitis) is a necrotizing inflammation of the blood vessels caused by various mechanisms (see Boxes 20.1 and 20.2). Necrosis of blood vessel walls leads to extravasation of RBCs (purpura), vascular obstruction, and tissue ischaemia or infarction. Vasculitis may affect vessels of different sizes in the skin, kidneys, gut, respiratory tract, peripheral nerves, and/or skeletal muscle  Vasculitis (angiitis) is a necrotizing inflammation of the blood vessels caused by various mechanisms (see Boxes 20.1 and 20.2). Necrosis of blood vessel walls leads to extravasation of RBCs (purpura), vascular obstruction, and tissue ischaemia or infarction. Vasculitis may affect vessels of different sizes in the skin, kidneys, gut, respiratory tract, peripheral nerves, and/or skeletal muscle  The histological findings in vasculitis may include:  The histological findings in vasculitis may include:  •    Perivascular neutrophilic inflammation extending into vessel walls •    Swelling and injury of endothelial cells. •    Perivascular neutrophilic inflammation extending into vessel walls •    Swelling and injury of endothelial cells. Symptoms and signs vary, but palpable purpura is the hallmark of a smallvessel cutaneous vasculitis (see Box 20.2, and Figs. 20.1 and 20.2).  Symptoms and signs vary, but palpable purpura is the hallmark of a smallvessel cutaneous vasculitis (see Box 20.2, and Figs. 20.1 and 20.2).  Clinical findings should be integrated with the results of serological, pathological, and imaging studies to reach a diagnosis. For classification criteria, see Box 20.1.  Clinical findings should be integrated with the results of serological, pathological, and imaging studies to reach a diagnosis. For classification criteria, see Box 20.1.
